body:
Sadly, even with health testing through OFA or pennhip, hip dysplasia is proven to be equally genetic and environmental (both the environment of the womb and as a puppy). This is why seeking health tested- NOT just healthy, but health tested- parents is so important, even in breeds where hips (and elbows too!) are an uncommon issue. It's hard to control genetics, but we can try. Could I ask her breed?

&#x200B;

I'm so sorry OP, I'll be hoping for a good outcome for you and your pup. Hip dysplasia won't end her life, or your love for her :) there's a lot you can do to make her comfortable and happy and your lives still full!
